ee-form prose.\n\nA core policy for paper writing is: do not hardcode result numbers inside plotting or calculation scripts when those values should be read from real artifacts. Read from files, derive from those inputs, and attach `@evidence[[...]]` refs that point back to the inputs.\n\n## Fixed directory structure\n\n```text\nscientific-evidence/\n  spec.md\n  bundle.manifest.json\n  ledger.jsonl\n  schemas/\n    evidence-reference.schema.json\n    artifact-manifest.schema.json\n    ledger-entry.schema.json\n    verification-report.schema.json\n```\n\n## Grammar summary\n\n- Evidence ref token: `@evidence[[TARGET]]` or `@evidence[[TARGET#LOCATOR]]`\n- `TARGET` may be a relative path, absolute path, URI, or stable identifier.\n- `LOCATOR` is opaque unless a verifier adapter knows how to interpret it.\n- Generated artifact sidecar name: `<artifact>.prov.json`\n- Central ledger file: `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl`\n\n## Schema set\n\nGenerate the canonical schema bundle with the script. The bundle includes:\n- `scientific-evidence/spec.md`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/evidence-reference.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/artifact-manifest.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/ledger-entry.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/verification-report.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/bundle.manifest.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl`\n\n## Examples\n\nPython:\n```python\n# Read the metric from disk; do not hardcode 96.7 here.\n# @evidence[[results/metrics.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\nimport json\npayload = json.loads(open(\"results/metrics.json\", encoding=\"utf-8\").read())\nvalue = payload[\"mnist\"][\"accuracy_mean\"]\n```\n\nBash:\n```bash\n# @evidence[[results/run_summary.csv#row(dataset=mnist,variant=proposed).accuracy]]\npython scripts/summarize_results.py --input results/run_summary.csv\n```\n\nLaTeX:\n```tex\nAccuracy reached 96.7\\%. % @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\n```\n\nMarkdown:\n```md\nMain result: 96.7%. <!-- @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]] -->\n```\n\nR:\n```r\n# @evidence[[results/summary.tsv#humanoid.edppo.mean]]\nsummary <- read.delim(\"results/summary.tsv\")\nvalue <- subset(summary, method == \"ED-PPO\" & env == \"Humanoid\")$mean[[1]]\n```\n\nYAML:\n```yaml\n# @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\nreported_metric: mnist_accuracy_mean\n```\n\nJSON sidecar:\n```json\n{\n  \"schema_name\": \"scientific-evidence.artifact-manifest\",\n  \"artifact\": {\"path\": \"figures/main.png\", \"kind\": \"figure\"},\n  \"derived_from\": [\n    \"@evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\",\n    \"@evidence[[scripts/build_figure.py]]\"\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Verification\n\nSmoke test the script, bundle generation, and verifier:\n```bash\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh --help\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh emit-bundle --root-dir /tmp/scientific-evidence-example\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh verify --root-dir /tmp/scientific-evidence-example\n```\n---\nname: memory-log\ndescription: Maintain a continuous, append-only `./memory.md` log of discoveries, evaluations, failures, and next hypotheses during agent runs.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nKeep durable run memory so idea evolution and program evidence are never lost between steps.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- At run start to initialize memory.\n- After every meaningful step (`idea`, `edit`, `evaluate`, `verify`, `paper`, `draft`).\n- Immediately after every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair.\n\n# What to do\n1. Initialize `./memory.md` if missing with these section headers:\n- `# Memory Log`\n- `## Context`\n- `## Idea Discoveries`\n- `## Program Evaluations`\n- `## Failures / Regressions`\n- `## Next Hypotheses`\n2. Also initialize global files if missing:\n- `./global_memory/memory.md`\n- `./global_memory/memory.jsonl`\n3. Never delete or rewrite prior entries; append only.\n4. For each appended entry, include:\n- timestamp\n- step type\n- change/discovery summary\n- concrete evidence\n- next action\n5. After every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair, append a `Program Evaluations` entry that includes:\n- `program_<idx>`\n- `combined_score`\n- `correct`\n- rubric pass/fail summary\n- `next_recommendation`\n6. For regressions or failed rubrics, append one root-cause hypothesis under `Failures / Regressions`.\n7. Before exit, append final status (`rubric_pass`, `cap_reached`, or task-specific stop reason) and next hypothesis under `Next Hypotheses`.\n8. When command execution is unavailable, use read/edit/write tools to append directly\nto the required markdown and JSONL files. Preserve all existing content. When command\nexecution is available, prefer the helper for deterministic dual-write:\n- script: detect the active agent config directory first, then use `<that-dir>/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py`\n- command template:\n```bash\nMEMORY_HELPER=\"$(find ./.claude ./.codex ./.cursor ./.opencode ./.gemini ./.pi -path '*/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py' -print -quit 2>/dev/null)\"\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" \\\n  --global-memory-md \"./global_memory/memory.md\" \\\n  --global-memory-jsonl \"./global_memory/memory.jsonl\" \\\n  --event-json '{\"step\":\"evaluate\",\"scope\":\"attempt\",\"summary\":\"...\",\"evidence\":\"...\",\"next_action\":\"...\",\"attempt_id\":\"{{ATTEMPT_ID}}\",\"idea_id\":\"{{IDEA_ID}}\",\"program_id\":\"program_2\",\"metrics\":{\"combined_score\":2.54,\"correct\":true}}'\n```\n9. JSONL event schema (required keys):\n- `timestamp` (auto-filled if omitted)\n- `step` (`idea|edit|evaluate|verify|paper|draft|final|conclusions_skip`)\n- `scope` (`idea|attempt|paper|campaign`)\n- `summary`\n- `evidence`\n- `next_action`\n- optional: `idea_id`, `attempt_id`, `program_id`, `metrics`, `rubrics`, `tags`\n\n10. Update distilled conclusions (`conclusions.md`).\n**Purpose.** `conclusions.md` is a *forward-looking guide* for the next worker (or your future self in a later cycle). Its job is to help readers (a) focus their effort on directions you've found promising, and (b) avoid spending compute on directions you've already shown to be unpromising. Each sentence should either point at a lead worth pursuing or warn against a dead end, framed so a researcher who has not read your `memory.md` can still act on it. Avoid chronology, restate-of-results, and post-mortem narration — those belong in `memory.md`. Conclusions are the synthesis layer that makes other workers' next cycles more efficient.\n\n**When.** After every memory-append in the cycle (idea / edit / evaluate / verify / paper / final), evaluate whether `conclusions.md` should be updated.\n\n**Update gate (the only valid triggers).**\n- (a) cycle **contradicts** an existing sentence → rewrite that sentence.\n- (b) cycle opens a **new category** not yet covered → add a sentence.\n- (c) cycle **flips a Do/Don't** recommendation → flip it.\n- (d) cycle **meaningfully refines** an existing sentence — narrows the conditions under which a rule holds, broadens its applicability to a config it wasn't known to apply to, sharpens a tentative claim into a confident one, or adds a qualifier the prior phrasing didn't cover → edit the sentence to incorporate the refinement. Simple re-confirmation in the same regime is **not** sufficient; you must identify the refinement dimension (narrow / broaden / sharpen / qualify) in `--gate-reason`.\n- otherwise → **keep**, do not invoke the helper.\n\n**Format constraints.** Single paragraph. ≤300 words. No blank lines (`\\n\\n`). Avoid detailed numerical figures — use qualitative thresholds (e.g. \"low LR\", \"small batch\") instead of precise floats like `LR=0.205`.\n\n**Update mechanism.** When execution is unavailable, apply the same validation rules\nand update the conclusions files directly with read/edit/write tools. When execution\nis available, use this helper invocation:\n```bash\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" --write-conclusions \\\n  --conclusions-md \"{{CONCLUSIONS_MD_FILE}}\" \\\n  --conclusions-history-jsonl \"{{CONCLUSIONS_HISTORY_FILE}}\" \\\n  --cycle-index {{CYCLE_INDEX}} \\\n  --worker-index {{WORKER_INDEX}} \\\n  --gate-trigger <a|b|c|d> \\\n  --gate-reason \"<one sentence; for (d) name the dimension>\" \\\n  --conclusions-text \"<the full new paragraph>\"\n```\n\n**Pre-flight self-check.** Before invoking the helper, count words (`len(paragraph.split())`) and confirm ≤300, and that the paragraph contains no blank line. Most validation rejections can be avoided here.\n\n**Retry budget — exactly 3 attempts per cycle.** On non-zero exit, read the stderr error, shorten the paragraph (drop the least-decisive sentence, merge two related sentences, or remove qualifiers), and retry. After **3 total attempts** with no success, the update is **discarded** for this cycle: do not retry further, do not write the rejected text anywhere, and append a single memory event with `step=\"conclusions_skip\"` whose `summary` names the last validation error. Underlying findings are not lost — they are already durable in `memory.md`.\n\n**Always log the gate decision.** Inside the regular memory append for the cycle, include `gate=<updated|kept|skipped>; trigger=<a|b|c|d|none>; reason=<one sentence>` in the entry's `summary` or `tags`. Use `skipped` only after the 3-attempt budget is exhausted.\n\n# Example evaluation entry\n```markdown\n### 2026-02-13T16:58:00Z | step=evaluate\n- program: program_3\n- combined_score: 2.54\n- correct: true\n- rubrics: uses_restarts=pass, constraint_optimization=pass, score_threshold=fail\n- evidence: late-stage overlap still visible in stdout trace\n- next_recommendation: raise late-stage overlap penalty and diversify restart seeds\n```\n---\nname: validate-paper\ndescription: Validate grounded paper artifacts and compile LaTeX PDF with strict fail-fast checks.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nRun final quality gate for the generated paper.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- After `paper.tex`, `references.bib`, and `claims_grounding.json` are written.\n- Before declaring final paper success.\n\n# What to do\n1. Validate sections and citation keys:\n```bash\npython scripts/validate_tex_refs.py \\\n  --tex paper.tex \\\n  --bib references.bib\n```\nRequire a substantive embedded Supplementary Information section after the bibliography. It must\nuse `\\\\appendix` and `\\\\section{Supplementary Information}`, contain grounded visible content plus\nat least one subsection, table, or figure, and contain no template placeholder text.\n2. Validate claim mappings, bibliography/provenance consistency, and every `@evidence[[...]]` link in the `scientific-evidence/` bundle:\n```bash\npython ../write-paper/scripts/validate_claims.py \\\n  --paper-data paper_data.json \\\n  --references references_provenance.json \\\n  --bib references.bib \\\n  --claims claims_grounding.json \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\nThis delegates to the `scientific-evidence` skill verifier, which classifies every `@evidence[[...]]` link as `resolved`, `anchored`, or `dangling`. Any `dangling` link (or an `@evidence[[...]]` token with no `scientific-evidence/` bundle present) is a hard error and must be sent back to the paper-writing agent for repair.\n3. Validate figure manifest and figure readability budget when `figure_manifest.json` is present or `paper_strategy.json` requires figures. The canonical schema is `templates/figure_manifest.schema.json`.\n```bash\npython .claude/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_figures.py \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\n4. Compile:\n```bash\nbash scripts/compile_latex.sh paper.tex\n```\nThe compile script also validates that Supplementary Information is visibly rendered on a page\nafter References with substantive content. Source-only structure checks are not sufficient for\nconditional or literal LaTeX constructs.\nIt records and validates every LaTeX input, restricts BibTeX to the canonical `references.bib`,\nand removes inherited Kpathsea path overrides before compiling.\n5. Require `paper.pdf` to exist.\n6. When `paper_data.json.paper_profile == \"standalone\"`, also fail on visible paper language that leaks pipeline terms such as `eligible`, `correct and eligible`, `correctness rate`, `worker_01`, `worker_02`, `combined_score`, or similar internal wording.\n7. When the selected format or venue guidance requires real author metadata, also fail on anonymous or generic placeholder author blocks and emit soft warnings when title/abstract/introduction/conclusion lack venue-relevant framing or when the discussion drifts into unrelated application narratives.\n8. Fail hard on any missing grounding link, missing section, missing/empty/misordered Supplementary Information, unresolved citation, bibliography/provenance mismatch, figure-manifest mismatch, density-budget violation, page-limit violation for formats with enforced budgets, standalone-language violation, author-block violation required by format/venue guidance, dangling `@evidence[[...]]` link, or compile error.\n9. Treat the parent orchestrator's validation report as authoritative during repair rounds. Last-body-page fill is rendered geometric vertical occupancy computed from text and graphics bounds, not character count or word density. Do not claim that page validation passed using a character-count proxy. If the canonical validator is unavailable in the agent workspace, make the targeted repair and return control so the parent can compile and revalidate it.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc 'cat paper_strategy.md; cat .codex/skills/memory-log/SKILL.md | head -85; rg --files .codex/skills | head -70; command -v apply_patch; command -v pdflatex; cat references.bib; head -65 paper.sty'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc 'cat paper_strategy.md; cat .codex/skills/memory-log/SKILL.md | head -85; rg --files .codex/skills | head -70; command -v apply_patch; command -v pdflatex; cat references.bib; head -65 paper.sty'","aggregated_output":"# Paper Framing Strategy\n\n## Decision\n\n**Ready — `paper_standalone`, ICLR 2026.** Provisional title: **Local Residual Transfer for Budgeted Embedding Benchmark Prediction**.\n\n**Central question:** How accurately can a sparse historical embedding-score matrix and one to eight queried scores predict a held-out model's sealed benchmark scores under model and heuristic-family holdouts, and how does disrupting historical score structure change that accuracy?\n\n**Central thesis:** On the pinned MTEB observed-score evaluation, the frozen low-rank predictor with cross-fitted local residual transfer has lower aggregate MAE than the five evaluated baselines and decreasing aggregate error across the tested budgets; historical-data disruptions expose limits to this advantage without isolating residual transfer's contribution.\n\nThis is one scoped empirical method study, not a search diary, a proof of optimal selection, or evidence about executing benchmarks on generative LLMs. The two holdouts are evidence blocks under one research question. A negative control result is part of the scientific conclusion, not discarded evidence.\n\n## Framing Alternatives and Input Impact\n\nThere is no resolved edit context, `previous_paper/`, prior manuscript, `aggregated/`, or `extra_tasks/` here. The JSON's `prior_paper_story_status: rejected` means rejecting an assumed inherited story, not claiming a manuscript was replaced. The original task is provenance, not permission to retain its broad generative-model scope.\n\n- **Search-success narrative: reject.** Selection order is not the scientific object, and development comparisons are not independent final comparisons.\n- **Shared cross-setting method-family narrative: reject.** Only one pinned embedding dataset is supported; its holdout/control conditions are not separate datasets or domains.\n- **Scoped prediction-and-limits narrative: select.** It links the available implementation, final comparisons, budget dependence, uncertainty and disruption controls without requiring new experiments.\n\n`paper_data.json.downstream_study` is absent. The independent `final_evaluation` is completed and qualified, grounded in `final.qualification.status` and `final.qualification.outcome`. Successful protocol completion is not proof of novelty, universal validity or superiority. No failed final qualification is present in this run. If a later audit retains metrics but reports `not_qualified`, that failure and unknown diagnostics must survive the abstract, main results, limitations, review and revisions; do not explain it as a performance or adaptivity failure without evaluator evidence.\n\nPrivate selection provenance directly identifies `results/ideas/02_cross_fitted_local_residual_medians/program_1/src/program.py` and its `RESIDUAL_METHOD.md` through `result.best_correct_score` and `results/best_program_manifest.json`. There is no new staged setting to privilege. The final MTEB manifest fixes revision `6dabbcc68c28ea5f180ce7df79347b3ca9cc6383`, 126 final-plane rows and 135 task/subset columns. These are text-embedding model results, not 135 independent benchmark suites or the full public leaderboard.\n\nReferences and provenance files exist, but metadata retrieval is not equivalent to reading the papers. No existing figure asset is assumed. This stage generates neither a manuscript nor figures.\n\n## Method Groups and Claim Boundaries\n\n1. **Studied method:** observed-entry ridge ALS; greedy all-column posterior-variance-reduction queries; new-row ridge inference; overlap-aware kernel-weighted cross-fitted residual medians; history-only tuning with an exact zero-blend fallback. Explain the evaluated package.\n2. **Low-rank baselines:** random-query and historical-variance-query low-rank prediction. Their different query policies prevent interpreting them as residual-only ablations.\n3. **Linear transfer baseline:** ridge regression from variance-ranked queried scores, with missing centered historical values filled by zero as implemented.\n4. **Simple references:** historical column means and a constant prediction equal to the new model's observed-score mean. Include both in the main table, including the weak reference.\n5. **Supporting conditions:** constant historical observed values and independent cyclic shifts of each column's value/mask pairs. These alter historical data, not a single estimator component.\n\nDescribe rank four, ridge 0.1, the source-defined fit/calibration caps and tuning grid from inspected code; do not present resource caps as measured time. Explain shared-anchor distances, donor overlap weights, finite observed donor residuals, weighted medians, effective-count shrinkage, and clipped outputs. Separate historical folds from the held-out new model. The selector sums gains over all columns; it is not the discarded self-excluding transfer-only selector and does not use revealed score values to choose subsequent indices.\n\nContribution claims should be limited to a fully specified estimator, an observed-support comparison with common sealed targets, and explicit disruption-based empirical boundaries. No matched final residual-off, unconditional-residual, donor-score-only or support-gating ablation is available. Training diagnostic code is not unbiased outer evidence. Do not claim residual correction causes the gain, optimal selection, new theory, or firstness. An algebraic explanation of the implemented estimator is useful; a new statistical guarantee is unsupported.\n\n## Results That Carry the Story\n\n### Primary Comparison\n\nUse a fixed-order main table with all five baselines and the studied method, showing aggregate and condition-specific sealed-target MAE. The selected method's retained overall values are:\n\n| Quantity | Retained value | Grounding source |\n| --- | ---: | --- |\n| MAE | 0.040807843083862115 | `final.metric.mae` |\n| Minimum aggregate baseline MAE | 0.045249969215420516 | `final.metric.baseline_mae` |\n| Baseline-minus-method MAE | 0.004442126131558401 | `final.metric.delta_best_baseline` |\n| RMSE | 0.06432104648676243 | `final.metric.rmse` |\n| Reported lower gain bound | 0.0021032442082077377 | `final.metric.gain_ci_low` |\n| Reported upper gain bound | 0.009199712663056218 | `final.metric.gain_ci_high` |\n\nThe baseline minimum is taken after overall aggregation, not selected independently for each row or budget. No baseline RMSE is retained in the comparison arrays, so do not invent it. Do not combine search scores with this table or replace the frozen selection post hoc.\n\n### Budget and Holdout Views\n\nThe pooled MAEs for budgets 1, 2, 4 and 8 are respectively 0.04523334197776794, 0.0422085090615931, 0.03963906108320536 and 0.03615046021288207 (`final.metric.mae_k1`, `final.metric.mae_k2`, `final.metric.mae_k4`, `final.metric.mae_k8`). Generate separate model-holdout and heuristic-family-holdout views from `final.evidence.episodes`; report each comparator rather than assuming superiority at every budget.\n\nThe three final seeds are 6841, 8089 and 9011. Seed variation is descriptive: rows and components recur, so these are not three independent datasets. Explain common sealed targets, uniform target averaging within row, equal row weights within episode, equal checkpoint weights, equal seed weights and equal primary-condition weights. The all-unqueried diagnostic has a query-dependent target set and is not the primary fair-comparison metric. A budget-response plot is a direct design comparison, not an optimization trajectory. No specified accuracy tolerance establishes that eight queries are sufficient.\n\n### Uncertainty and Controls\n\nThe evaluator uses 400 paired component multinomial resamples with RNG 424242 and shared multiplicities across methods, conditions and seeds. It forms five baseline-specific intervals using quantiles 0.005/0.995 under its Bonferroni construction, then reports minima of the lower bounds and minima of the upper bounds. State this exact construction rather than labeling the supplied pair as a conventional single-comparator 95% interval. Explain finite bootstrap resolution and dependence. Candidate self-diagnostics remain unverified; the row/checkpoint losses here are evaluator-computed, as checked in `task/evaluate_final.py`.\n\nConstant-history MAE is 0.06436711309859705 (`final.metric.constant_control_mae`); column-shifted-history MAE is 0.06856952089296987 (`final.metric.permuted_control_mae`). Compare controls against their matched model-holdout condition, not the pooled two-condition primary result. Retained arrays show no advantage over the column-mean reference under column-shifted history. Keep this boundary in the main results and conclusion. Constant observed values preserve missingness; shifts preserve each column's value/mask marginals while breaking row alignment. Neither manipulation isolates residual transport nor proves a causal or hidden-adaptivity explanation.\n\n## Figures, Readability and Placement\n\nThe inspected `paper.sty` sets a **single 5.5-inch text column** (about 397.5 TeX points) and a 9-inch text height. Full-width and column-width have the same maximum physical span; do not assume a two-column `figure*` layout. Legacy comments in the style do not establish official ICLR 2026 provenance; verify that separately before claiming format compliance.\n\n| Figure | Specification | Placement and footprint | Readability budget |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `method_overview` | Required diagram via `generate-concept-figure`; distinguish history-only fitting/calibration from held-out querying and sealed targets; combine base prediction and local residual correction without fake numeric cells. | Page 1 or 2; full 5.5-inch width; two vertically stacked panels; artwork 2.0–2.3 inches high plus caption, about one third page. | Two panels, one per row; at most five labeled objects per panel and two information streams; at least 9-point text. |\n| `budget_comparison` | Required matplotlib direct budget-response plot, separate model and heuristic-family holdout panels; series are studied method, random-query low-rank, variance-query low-rank, linear ridge. | Pages 5–6; full 5.5-inch width; two panels side by side; artwork 2.4–2.7 inches high plus caption, about 0.4 page. | Two panels, four categories and four series per panel; common axes and external legend; at least 8.5-point text. Stack rather than shrink if labels collide. |\n| `history_structure_controls` | Required matplotlib grouped-point comparison; Intact / Constant / Column-shifted conditions; studied method, column means and variance-query low-rank; all under the matched model-holdout protocol. | Pages 6–7; 0.9 text width, about 4.95 inches; artwork 1.7–2.0 inches high plus caption, about 0.3 page. | One panel, three conditions, three series; at least 9-point labels; offset coincident points without changing values. |\n\nAll figures have `allow_progression: false`. At most one overview uses the concept-figure workflow; later orchestration owns that rendering. Explain short labels in captions, use consistent method colors and avoid an architecture drawing that invents neural components. The overview's categories are qualitative encodings, not simulated measurements.\n\nGenerate quantitative figures from retained arrays using a reproducible plotting script, exporting **PDF and PNG at 300 DPI**. LaTeX includes the PDF whenever one exists. Raster-only full-width figures must be at least 2400 pixels wide; a genuinely reduced column figure must be at least 1200 pixels wide. Regenerate from source; never upscale. Preserve editable overview source and prompt provenance. Any seed error bars must be explicitly descriptive standard deviations or otherwise defined uncertainty, never implied independent-sample intervals.\n\n## Body Plan Without Padding\n\nTarget **8.5 substantive body pages**, excluding references and appendix, inside the venue's 8–9-page requirement. An eight-page body requires its last page at least 75% full. Approximate space allocations include all embedded figures, tables and captions:\n\n- Title, abstract and introduction: 1.25 pages.\n- Related work: 0.75 page.\n- Problem and information boundary: 0.75 page.\n- Method and historical calibration: 1.75 pages.\n- Evaluation protocol and uncertainty: 1.25 pages.\n- Results across budgets, holdouts and controls: 1.75 pages.\n- Discussion, limitations and conclusion: 1.0 page.\n\nSuggested headings are Introduction; Related Work; Budgeted Score Prediction and Local Residual Transfer; Evaluation Protocol; Accuracy Across Budgets and Holdouts; Historical-Structure Controls; Discussion and Limitations; Conclusion. The allocations describe content, not rigid page breaks.\n\nUse space for the observed-entry ALS objective, new-row inference and correction equations, a compact algorithm box and parameter table, careful fixed-target reasoning, complete comparator reporting, bootstrap interpretation and sharp failure conditions. Do not fill space with a campaign account, irrelevant literature, unmeasured ablations or stretched formatting. If grounded depth cannot sustain the minimum, report that rather than padding.\n\n## Supplementary Information After References\n\nThe supplement adds implementation and evidence depth, not hidden central limitations. Its source IDs are existing entries in `paper_data.json.grounding_sources`; the local method and evaluator paths below are direct provenance, not invented IDs.\n\n1. **Observed-score extraction and split provenance.** Use the final manifest, plan and evaluator for pinned results/task-definition revisions, metric identity, coverage, normalization, duplicate components, heuristic families and disjoint allocation. Explain that current CC0 attribution does not establish historical licensing of a snapshot without a LICENSE file. Sources: `final.evidence.episodes`, `final.qualification.status`, `final.qualification.outcome` plus direct local provenance.\n2. **Residual-transfer algorithm and historical calibration.** Give pseudocode, the tuning grid, two-level historical folds, donor filtering and weighting, effective counts, fallbacks and computational caps from selected source. Explain approximate auxiliary fits and why cross-fitting alone guarantees neither unbiased residuals nor superiority. Sources: `result.best_correct_score`, `final.evidence.episodes` plus inspected `src/program.py` and `RESIDUAL_METHOD.md`.\n3. **Complete sealed-target comparisons and paired uncertainty.** Derive all-method condition/budget/seed tables from retained arrays, with evaluator method order and aggregation/resampling pseudocode. Keep primary values and uncertainty interpretation in the body. Sources: `final.evidence.episodes`, `final.metric.mae`, `final.metric.baseline_mae`, `final.metric.gain_ci_low`, `final.metric.gain_ci_high`.\n4. **Changing-target diagnostic and disruption details.** Compare retained all-unqueried and sealed-target losses, marking changing targets clearly; document mask-preserving constant values and column value/mask shifts. Do not claim an unmeasured missingness ablation. Sources: `final.evidence.episodes`, `final.metric.constant_control_mae`, `final.metric.permuted_control_mae`.\n5. **Reproducibility, configuration selection and incomplete evidence.** Map future extraction/plot scripts to every figure and table, document existing evaluator entry point, requirements, final seeds, hashes and reset result. Briefly disclose adaptive search and the failed support-gating attempt with no retained numeric performance from `results/attempts_history.jsonl`. Its missing causal diagnostics remain unknown. Search alternatives are not matched final ablations. Sources: `result.best_correct_score`, `result.top_program_01`, `result.top_program_02`, `result.total_attempts`, `result.total_evaluated_programs`, `final.evidence.episodes`, `final.metric.reset_max_abs_delta` plus direct attempt records. No `study.*` sources exist; do not invent study counts or treat ambiguous aggregate labels as scored/unscored diagnoses.\n6. **LLM Usage.** Provide the venue-required separate disclosure of documented ideation, implementation assistance, query planning, framing, and later writing assistance when performed. Numerical evidence comes from the evaluator, not generated measurements. Use the documented interaction record and `final.evidence.episodes`; do not claim later work is already complete.\n\nNo later posthoc ranking is guessed. Any authoritative appendix-ranked analyses arriving later belong alongside this plan only when grounded.\n\n## Limitations That Must Stay Visible\n\nPrediction is evaluated on previously observed cells within frozen queryable reservoirs, not genuinely unreported scores. Missingness may be informative. Task/subset columns are heterogeneous and dependent; heuristic family labels cannot certify undisclosed ancestry. Repeated seeds reuse data and components. There are no matched outer residual-component ablations, temporal tests, cross-leaderboard tests, generative-model tests or established deployment-cost savings. The historical-source licensing caveat remains. The discarded selector and support-gating variants do not inherit the selected method's results.\n\nPreserve the independently reported final outcome. Reader-facing scientific descriptions should explain information access, common targets and replay checks, not equate successful evaluation with an exhaustive validity guarantee or a performance threshold. Protocol outcomes and statistical outcomes are distinct only to the extent evaluator evidence identifies them.\n\n## Wording and Citation Guardrails\n\nAvoid headings centered on Search Progression, Campaign Phases, Research Timeline, Milestones, First Successful Result, Experiment A / Experiment B, Round-by-Round Improvements, or Winner Selection as the Main Result.\n\nAvoid phrases such as “first valid result,” “subsequent improvements,” “we then tried,” “later variants,” and “next-best.” The following are private-only internal labels, never visible paper metrics: `correctness rate`, `correct and eligible`, `eligible`, `worker_01`, `worker_02`, `combined_score`, `rubric`. Also forbid unsupported claims of “state of the art,” “provably optimal benchmark selection,” “residual correction explains the gain,” “eight benchmarks are enough,” “generalizes to generative LLM evaluation,” and “unscored attempts were worse.”\n\nOnly cite keys already in `references.bib` with synchronized provenance and actually consulted content. Existing matrix-factorization/neighbor-method metadata gives candidate positioning, not permission to invent findings. Do not pad related work with unrelated recommender applications or make an unverified novelty claim. Approved starter works require permissible consultation and grounded retrieval before use. Never search for or infer the prohibited paper or its citation network; stop and record any accidental encounter.\n\nKeep the manuscript double-blind: omit names, affiliations, acknowledgements, funding statements and identifying links. Internal paths remain private grounding metadata, not public prose. Do not claim new experiments, completed plots, final manuscript drafting or final-paper validation at this stage.\n---\nname: memory-log\ndescription: Maintain a continuous, append-only `./memory.md` log of discoveries, evaluations, failures, and next hypotheses during agent runs.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nKeep durable run memory so idea evolution and program evidence are never lost between steps.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- At run start to initialize memory.\n- After every meaningful step (`idea`, `edit`, `evaluate`, `verify`, `paper`, `draft`).\n- Immediately after every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair.\n\n# What to do\n1. Initialize `./memory.md` if missing with these section headers:\n- `# Memory Log`\n- `## Context`\n- `## Idea Discoveries`\n- `## Program Evaluations`\n- `## Failures / Regressions`\n- `## Next Hypotheses`\n2. Also initialize global files if missing:\n- `./global_memory/memory.md`\n- `./global_memory/memory.jsonl`\n3. Never delete or rewrite prior entries; append only.\n4. For each appended entry, include:\n- timestamp\n- step type\n- change/discovery summary\n- concrete evidence\n- next action\n5. After every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair, append a `Program Evaluations` entry that includes:\n- `program_<idx>`\n- `combined_score`\n- `correct`\n- rubric pass/fail summary\n- `next_recommendation`\n6. For regressions or failed rubrics, append one root-cause hypothesis under `Failures / Regressions`.\n7. Before exit, append final status (`rubric_pass`, `cap_reached`, or task-specific stop reason) and next hypothesis under `Next Hypotheses`.\n8. When command execution is unavailable, use read/edit/write tools to append directly\nto the required markdown and JSONL files. Preserve all existing content. When command\nexecution is available, prefer the helper for deterministic dual-write:\n- script: detect the active agent config directory first, then use `<that-dir>/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py`\n- command template:\n```bash\nMEMORY_HELPER=\"$(find ./.claude ./.codex ./.cursor ./.opencode ./.gemini ./.pi -path '*/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py' -print -quit 2>/dev/null)\"\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" \\\n  --global-memory-md \"./global_memory/memory.md\" \\\n  --global-memory-jsonl \"./global_memory/memory.jsonl\" \\\n  --event-json '{\"step\":\"evaluate\",\"scope\":\"attempt\",\"summary\":\"...\",\"evidence\":\"...\",\"next_action\":\"...\",\"attempt_id\":\"{{ATTEMPT_ID}}\",\"idea_id\":\"{{IDEA_ID}}\",\"program_id\":\"program_2\",\"metrics\":{\"combined_score\":2.54,\"correct\":true}}'\n```\n9. JSONL event schema (required keys):\n- `timestamp` (auto-filled if omitted)\n- `step` (`idea|edit|evaluate|verify|paper|draft|final|conclusions_skip`)\n- `scope` (`idea|attempt|paper|campaign`)\n- `summary`\n- `evidence`\n- `next_action`\n- optional: `idea_id`, `attempt_id`, `program_id`, `metrics`, `rubrics`, `tags`\n\n10. Update distilled conclusions (`conclusions.md`).\n**Purpose.** `conclusions.md` is a *forward-looking guide* for the next worker (or your future self in a later cycle). Its job is to help readers (a) focus their effort on directions you've found promising, and (b) avoid spending compute on directions you've already shown to be unpromising. Each sentence should either point at a lead worth pursuing or warn against a dead end, framed so a researcher who has not read your `memory.md` can still act on it. Avoid chronology, restate-of-results, and post-mortem narration — those belong in `memory.md`. Conclusions are the synthesis layer that makes other workers' next cycles more efficient.\n\n**When.** After every memory-append in the cycle (idea / edit / evaluate / verify / paper / final), evaluate whether `conclusions.md` should be updated.\n\n**Update gate (the only valid triggers).**\n- (a) cycle **contradicts** an existing sentence → rewrite that sentence.\n- (b) cycle opens a **new category** not yet covered → add a sentence.\n- (c) cycle **flips a Do/Don't** recommendation → flip it.\n- (d) cycle **meaningfully refines** an existing sentence — narrows the conditions under which a rule holds, broadens its applicability to a config it wasn't known to apply to, sharpens a tentative claim into a confident one, or adds a qualifier the prior phrasing didn't cover → edit the sentence to incorporate the refinement. Simple re-confirmation in the same regime is **not** sufficient; you must identify the refinement dimension (narrow / broaden / sharpen / qualify) in `--gate-reason`.\n- otherwise → **keep**, do not invoke the helper.\n\n**Format constraints.** Single paragraph. ≤300 words. No blank lines (`\\n\\n`). Avoid detailed numerical figures — use qualitative thresholds (e.g. \"low LR\", \"small batch\") instead of precise floats like `LR=0.205`.\n\n**Update mechanism.** When execution is unavailable, apply the same validation rules\nand update the conclusions files directly with read/edit/write tools. When execution\nis available, use this helper invocation:\n```bash\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" --write-conclusions \\\n  --conclusions-md \"{{CONCLUSIONS_MD_FILE}}\" \\\n  --conclusions-history-jsonl \"{{CONCLUSIONS_HISTORY_FILE}}\" \\\n  --cycle-index {{CYCLE_INDEX}} \\\n  --worker-index {{WORKER_INDEX}} \\\n  --gate-trigger <a|b|c|d> \\\n  --gate-reason \"<one sentence; for (d) name the dimension>\" \\\n.codex/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh\n.codex/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.py\n.codex/skills/scientific-evidence/SKILL.md\n.codex/skills/memory-log/SKILL.md\n.codex/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py\n.codex/skills/retrieve-references/scripts/openalex_fetch.py\n.codex/skills/retrieve-references/scripts/scholarly_fetch.py\n.codex/skills/retrieve-references/scripts/s2_fetch.py\n.codex/skills/retrieve-references/templates/provenance.schema.json\n.codex/skills/retrieve-references/SKILL.md\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/SKILL.md\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_compile_inputs.py\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/compile_latex.sh\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_figures.py\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_rendered_si.py\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_tex_refs.py\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/templates/figure_manifest.schema.json\n.codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/SKILL.md\n.codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/scripts/validate_claims.py\n.codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/templates/claims_grounding.schema.json\n.codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/templates/alife/alifeconf.sty\n.codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/templates/alife/paper.tex\n.codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/templates/references.bib\n.codex/skills/generate-concept-figure/SKILL.md\n.codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/templates/iclr/paper.tex\n.codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/templates/iclr/paper.sty\n.codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/templates/iclr/paper.bst\n/usr/bin/pdflatex\n@Article{Takács2008MatrixFA,\n author = {G. Takács and István Pilászy and B. Németh and D. Tikk},\n booktitle = {ACM Conference on Recommender Systems},\n pages = {267-274},\n title = {Matrix factorization and neighbor based algorithms for the netflix prize problem},\n year = {2008}\n}\n\n@Article{Ranjbar2015AnIM,\n author = {Manizheh Ranjbar and P. Moradi and Mostafa Azami and M. Jalili},\n booktitle = {Engineering applications of artificial intelligence},\n journal = {Eng. Appl. Artif. Intell.},\n pages = {58-66},\n title = {An imputation-based matrix factorization method for improving accuracy of collaborative filtering systems},\n volume = {46},\n year = {2015}\n}\n\n@Article{Awan2021ARE,\n author = {Mazhar Javed Awan and R. Khan and Haitham Nobanee and Awais Yasin and S. Anwar and Usman Naseem and Vishwa Pratap Singh},\n booktitle = {Electronics},\n journal = {Electronics},\n pages = {1215},\n title = {A Recommendation Engine for Predicting Movie Ratings Using a Big Data Approach},\n volume = {10},\n year = {2021}\n}\n\n@Article{Kannan2014BoundedMF,\n author = {Ramakrishnan Kannan and M. Ishteva and Haesun Park},\n booktitle = {Knowledge and Information Systems},\n journal = {Knowledge and Information Systems},\n pages = {491-511},\n title = {Bounded matrix factorization for recommender system},\n volume = {39},\n year = {2014}\n}\n\n@Article{Parvin2019ASA,\n author = {Hashem Parvin and P. Moradi and Shahrokh Esmaeili and N. Qader},\n booktitle = {Knowledge-Based Systems},\n journal = {Knowl. Based Syst.},\n pages = {92-107},\n title = {A scalable and robust trust-based nonnegative matrix factorization recommender using the alternating direction method},\n volume = {166},\n year = {2019}\n}\n\n@Article{Dakhel2018ASR,\n author = {Arghavan Moradi Dakhel and Hadi Tabatabaee Malazi and M. Mahdavi},\n booktitle = {Applied intelligence (Boston)},\n journal = {Applied Intelligence},\n pages = {527-540},\n title = {A social recommender system using item asymmetric correlation},\n volume = {48},\n year = {2018}\n}\n\n@Article{Paleti2020ApproachingTC,\n author = {Lakshmikanth Paleti and P. R. Krishna and J. V. R. Murthy},\n booktitle = {Evolutionary Intelligence},\n journal = {Evolutionary Intelligence},\n pages = {835 - 849},\n title = {Approaching the cold-start problem using community detection based alternating least square factorization in recommendation systems},\n volume = {14},\n year = {2020}\n}\n\n@Article{Zhang2012InducibleRF,\n author = {Zhen-Yue Zhang and Keke Zhao and H. Zha},\n booktitle = {Neurocomputing},\n journal = {Neurocomputing},\n pages = {52-62},\n title = {Inducible regularization for low-rank matrix factorizations for collaborative filtering},\n volume = {97},\n year = {2012}\n}\n\n@Article{Deng2019PSLEP,\n author = {Wei-Xin Deng and Peng-Yu Wang and Jing Wang and Chao Li and M. Guo},\n booktitle = {BenchCouncil International Symposium},\n pages = {101-109},\n title = {PSL: Exploiting Parallelism, Sparsity and Locality to Accelerate Matrix Factorization on x86 Platforms},\n year = {2019}\n}\n\n@Article{Natarajan2022CDSemMFCS,\n author = {S. Natarajan and S. Vairavasundaram and K. Kotecha and I. V. and S. Palani and Jatinderkumar R. Saini and L. Ravi},\n booktitle = {IEEE Access},\n journal = {IEEE Access},\n pages = {1-1},\n title = {CD-SemMF: Cross-Domain Semantic Relatedness Based Matrix Factorization Model Enabled with Linked Open Data for User Cold Start Issue},\n volume = {PP},\n year = {2022}\n}\n\n@Inproceedings{June2013ColdSR,\n author = {Martin Saveski June},\n title = {Cold Start Recommendations : A Non-negative Matrix Factorization Approach},\n year = {2013}\n}\n\n@Article{Efrizoni2025OptimizationOC,\n author = {Lusiana Efrizoni and Junadhi Junadhi and Agustin Agustin},\n booktitle = {Matrik},\n journal = {MATRIK : Jurnal Manajemen, Teknik Informatika dan Rekayasa Komputer},\n title = {Optimization of Content Recommendation System Based on User Preferences Using Neural Collaborative Filtering},\n year = {2025}\n}\n\n@Conference{Priyati2022TheCS,\n author = {Avis Priyati and A. Laksito and H. Sismoro},\n booktitle = {2022 5th International Conference on Information and Communications Technology (ICOIACT)},\n journal = {2022 5th International Conference on Information and Communications Technology (ICOIACT)},\n pages = {177-182},\n title = {The Comparison Study of Matrix Factorization on Collaborative Filtering Recommender System},\n year = {2022}\n}\n\n@Conference{Parvin2018NonnegativeMF,\n author = {Hashem Parvin and P. Moradi and Shahrokh Esmaeili},\n booktitle = {Electrical Engineering (ICEE), Iranian Conference on},\n journal = {Electrical Engineering (ICEE), Iranian Conference on},\n pages = {1571-1576},\n title = {Nonnegative Matrix Factorization Regularized with Trust Relationships for Solving Cold-Start Problem in Recommender Systems},\n year = {2018}\n}\n\n@Inproceedings{Parvin2020CombiningNM,\n author = {Parvin and Moradi and Sh and Esmaeili},\n title = {Combining Nonnegative Matrix Factorization technique with Trust Relationships for Recommendation in Social Networks},\n year = {2020}\n}\n\n@Article{Akimchuk2019EvaluatingRS,\n author = {Danny Akimchuk and Timothy Clerico and D. Turnbull},\n booktitle = {arXiv.org},\n journal = {ArXiv},\n title = {Evaluating Recommender System Algorithms for Generating Local Music Playlists},\n volume = {abs/1907.08687},\n year = {2019}\n}\n\n@Inproceedings{Kampffmeyer2015ParallelizationOT,\n author = {Michael C. Kampffmeyer},\n title = {Parallelization of the Alternating-Least-Squares Algorithm With Weighted Regularization for Efficient GPU Execution in Recommender Systems},\n year = {2015}\n}\n\n@Inproceedings{Artukarslan2019CollaborativeFB,\n author = {Merve Artukarslan and S. E. Alptekin},\n title = {Collaborative Filtering Based Recommender System Design For E-Commerce: A Case Study},\n year = {2019}\n}\n\n@Conference{Ming2022ImprovingCF,\n author = {Low Jia Ming and C. Lim and I. Tan},\n booktitle = {Asia-Pacific Signal and Information Processing Association Annual Summit and Conference},\n journal = {2022 Asia-Pacific Signal and Information Processing Association Annual Summit and Conference (APSIPA ASC)},\n pages = {990-996},\n title = {Improving co-SVD for cold-start recommendations using sparsity reduction},\n year = {2022}\n}\n\n@Article{Kannan2013BoundedMF,\n author = {Ramakrishnan Kannan and M. Ishteva and Haesun Park},\n booktitle = {Knowledge and Information Systems},\n journal = {Knowledge and Information Systems},\n pages = {491 - 511},\n title = {Bounded matrix factorization for recommender system},\n volume = {39},\n year = {2013}\n}\n\n@Inproceedings{Mensch2017LearningFM,\n author = {A. Mensch and Julien Mairal and B. Thirion and Gaël Varoquaux},\n title = {Learning for Massive Matrix Factorization},\n year = {2017}\n}\n\n@Conference{Aung2015AlternatingLS,\n author = {T. Aung and Rachsuda Jiamthapthaksin},\n booktitle = {International Joint Conference on Computer Science and Software Engineering},\n journal = {2015 12th International Joint Conference on Computer Science and Software Engineering (JCSSE)},\n pages = {297-302},\n title = {Alternating Least Squares with Incremental Learning Bias},\n year = {2015}\n}\n\n@Inproceedings{Daskalov2011IterativeMF,\n author = {B. Daskalov},\n title = {Iterative methods for matrix factorization with missing data},\n year = {2011}\n}\n\n@Article{Yang2015MatrixFR,\n author = {Qian Yang},\n journal = {Journal of Chongqing University of Arts and Sciences},\n title = {Matrix factorization recommender algorithm using trust propagation},\n year = {2015}\n}\n\n@Article{Salako2026LatentGO,\n author = {Joshua Salako},\n booktitle = {arXiv.org},\n journal = {ArXiv},\n title = {Latent Geometry of Taste: Scalable Low-Rank Matrix Factorization for Recommender Systems},\n volume = {abs/2601.03466},\n year = {2026}\n}\n%%%% ICLR Macros (LaTex)\n%%%% Adapted by Hugo Larochelle from the NIPS stylefile Macros\n%%%% Style File\n%%%% Dec 12, 1990   Rev Aug 14, 1991; Sept, 1995; April, 1997; April, 1999; October 2014\n\n% This file can be used with Latex2e whether running in main mode, or\n% 2.09 compatibility mode.\n%\n% If using main mode, you need to include the commands\n%             \\documentclass{article}\n%             \\usepackage{iclr14submit_e,times}\n%\n\n% Change the overall width of the page.  If these parameters are\n%       changed, they will require corresponding changes in the\n%       maketitle section.\n%\n\\usepackage{eso-pic} % used by \\AddToShipoutPicture\n\\RequirePackage{fancyhdr}\n\\RequirePackage{natbib}\n\n% modification to natbib citations\n\\setcitestyle{authoryear,round,citesep={;},aysep={,},yysep={;}}\n\n\\renewcommand{\\topfraction}{0.95}   % let figure take up nearly whole page\n\\renewcommand{\\textfraction}{0.05}  % let figure take up nearly whole page\n\n% Define iclrfinal, set to true if iclrfinalcopy is defined\n\\newif\\ificlrfinal\n\\iclrfinalfalse\n\\def\\iclrfinalcopy{\\iclrfinaltrue}\n\\font\\iclrtenhv  = phvb at 8pt\n\n% Specify the dimensions of each page\n\n\\setlength{\\paperheight}{11in}\n\\setlength{\\paperwidth}{8.5in}\n\n\n\\oddsidemargin .5in    %   Note \\oddsidemargin = \\evensidemargin\n\\evensidemargin .5in\n\\marginparwidth 0.07 true in\n%\\marginparwidth 0.75 true in\n%\\topmargin 0 true pt           % Nominal distance from top of page to top of\n%\\topmargin 0.125in\n\\topmargin -0.625in\n\\addtolength{\\headsep}{0.25in}\n\\textheight 9.0 true in       % Height of text (including footnotes & figures)\n\\textwidth 5.5 true in        % Width of text line.\n\\widowpenalty=10000\n\\clubpenalty=10000\n\n% \\thispagestyle{empty}        \\pagestyle{empty}\n\\flushbottom \\sloppy\n\n% We're never going to need a table of contents, so just flush it to\n% save space --- suggested by drstrip@sandia-2\n\\def\\addcontentsline#1#2#3{}\n\n% Title stuff, taken from deproc.\n\\def\\maketitle{\\par\n\\begingroup\n   \\def\\thefootnote{\\fnsymbol{footnote}}\n   \\def\\@makefnmark{\\hbox to 0pt{$^{\\@thefnmark}$\\hss}} % for perfect author\n                                                        % name centering\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
