{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc 'rg --files .codex/skills | sort; cat paper_strategy.json'","aggregated_output":".codex/skills/generate-concept-figure/SKILL.md\n.codex/skills/memory-log/SKILL.md\n.codex/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py\n.codex/skills/retrieve-references/SKILL.md\n.codex/skills/retrieve-references/scripts/openalex_fetch.py\n.codex/skills/retrieve-references/scripts/s2_fetch.py\n.codex/skills/retrieve-references/scripts/scholarly_fetch.py\n.codex/skills/retrieve-references/templates/provenance.schema.json\n.codex/skills/scientific-evidence/SKILL.md\n.codex/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.py\n.codex/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/SKILL.md\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/compile_latex.sh\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_compile_inputs.py\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_figures.py\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_rendered_si.py\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_tex_refs.py\n.codex/skills/validate-paper/templates/figure_manifest.schema.json\n.codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/SKILL.md\n.codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/scripts/validate_claims.py\n.codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/templates/alife/alifeconf.sty\n.codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/templates/alife/paper.tex\n.codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/templates/claims_grounding.schema.json\n.codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/templates/iclr/paper.bst\n.codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/templates/iclr/paper.sty\n.codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/templates/iclr/paper.tex\n.codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/templates/references.bib\n{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"selected_prompt_family\": \"paper_standalone\",\n  \"recommended_title\": \"Local Residual Transfer for Budgeted Embedding Benchmark Prediction\",\n  \"central_question\": \"How accurately can a sparse historical embedding-score matrix and one to eight queried scores predict a held-out model's sealed benchmark scores under model and heuristic-family holdouts, and how does disrupting historical score structure change that accuracy?\",\n  \"central_thesis\": \"On the pinned MTEB observed-score evaluation, the frozen low-rank predictor with cross-fitted local residual transfer has lower aggregate MAE than the five evaluated baselines and decreasing aggregate error across the tested query budgets, while history-disruption controls expose limits to this advantage without isolating the contribution of residual transfer.\",\n  \"narrative_rationale\": \"The strongest evidence is the completed independent final evaluation, not the search ordering: MAE 0.040807843083862115 versus the minimum aggregate baseline MAE 0.045249969215420516, with budgets 1, 2, 4, 8 and two primary holdout conditions across three seeds. The final evidence also contains evaluator-computed row/checkpoint losses for all five baselines and matched constant-history and column-shift controls. This supports one scoped empirical method study, not a proof of optimal benchmark selection, a residual-component superiority claim, or a generative-LLM claim. Dataset conditions are evidence blocks under one question, not separate discoveries. No downstream_study object is present; final evidence was read independently.\",\n  \"prior_paper_story_status\": \"rejected\",\n  \"edit_framing_decision\": \"Fresh framing, not an edit-mode replacement: no resolved edit-context files, previous_paper directory, prior manuscript, aggregated directory, or extra_tasks directory is available. The rejected option is importing an assumed prior story from memory; there is no prior manuscript to preserve. Compare candidate framings explicitly: a search-success story is rejected as chronological and nonconfirmatory; a broad cross-setting method-family story is rejected because only one pinned embedding dataset is evaluated; a scoped prediction-and-limits story is selected because it matches the final evidence and selected implementation.\",\n  \"staged_input_impact\": [\n    \"The selected implementation is directly available at results/ideas/02_cross_fitted_local_residual_medians/program_1/src/program.py with RESIDUAL_METHOD.md; result.best_correct_score and results/best_program_manifest.json identify this selection. Mechanism description is grounded by inspection, not inferred from the variant name.\",\n    \"The final manifest and evaluator establish a 126-row, 135-task/subset-column final plane from the pinned MTEB snapshot; this narrows the original broad language-model motivation to text-embedding score prediction. These are not 135 independent benchmark suites or the entire public leaderboard.\",\n    \"Final row/checkpoint arrays permit direct budget, holdout and control comparisons; absent outer component ablations prevent claiming residual correction causes the observed gain. Search-only alternatives do not become matched final ablations.\",\n    \"final.qualification.status is completed and final.qualification.outcome is qualified. This is a protocol/evaluation outcome, not proof of novelty, generalization, or performance at every budget. No failed final qualification is present in this run; any later not_qualified outcome must remain disclosed even if metrics are retained.\",\n    \"The references files are present, but available retrieval metadata is not a substitute for reading a paper or support for detailed related-work claims; figure assets are currently absent. This stage plans figures rather than claiming they exist.\"\n  ],\n  \"method_groups\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Low-rank prediction with cross-fitted local residual transfer\",\n      \"role\": \"proposed\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"The frozen studied method: observed-entry ridge ALS, greedy all-column posterior-variance reduction for querying, ridge inference for the new row, and overlap-aware kernel-weighted donor residual medians with history-only tuning and zero-blend fallback. Describe the full evaluated configuration rather than attributing its performance to one unablated component.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Low-rank prediction with random or variance-ranked queries\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Two evaluator-run comparators share the low-rank prediction family but use random or historical-column-variance query ordering. They contextualize the evaluated package, not a clean residual-only or query-only ablation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Linear ridge transfer\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"The evaluator fits ridge transfer from queried scores to all columns using observed historical values with missing centered entries filled by zero; its variance-ranked queries and distinct fitting procedure must be stated. Retain this fixed comparator in main results without inventing an unevaluated matched greedy-ALS baseline.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Column means and observed-score mean\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"The two simple references distinguish a query-free historical prior from copying the new model's queried-score mean across targets. Include both in the main numeric comparison even when the latter is weak; do not omit unfavorable or uninteresting baseline evidence.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Constant-history and independently column-shifted history\",\n      \"role\": \"supporting\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"These are matched data-disruption conditions, not new methods or isolated component ablations. Constant observed values preserve masks; cyclic shifts of each column's value/mask pairs preserve column marginals while disrupting row alignment. They establish a descriptive boundary on the intact-history results, not causal identification or a hidden-adaptivity test.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"results_focus\": [\n    \"Main table: all five baseline methods and the studied method, in a predefined scientific grouping, with overall fixed-target MAE and condition-specific MAE. Report the final RMSE 0.06432104648676243 only for the studied method unless comparator RMSE is actually available; never manufacture baseline RMSE.\",\n    \"Primary aggregate: final.metric.mae = 0.040807843083862115; final.metric.baseline_mae = 0.045249969215420516; final.metric.delta_best_baseline = 0.004442126131558401. The baseline minimum is chosen once after the same overall aggregation, not separately per row, budget or condition. Keep search scores out of this table.\",\n    \"Budget comparison: aggregate MAEs at k=1/2/4/8 are 0.04523334197776794, 0.0422085090615931, 0.03963906108320536, 0.03615046021288207. Plot separate model-holdout and heuristic-family-holdout panels from final.evidence.episodes with the same axes. These are observation-budget comparisons, not optimization trajectories; do not infer that eight queries are sufficient for an unspecified tolerance.\",\n    \"Uncertainty: report seed-level values descriptively and preserve paired component resampling. The supplied gain bounds 0.0021032442082077377 and 0.009199712663056218 are minima across five baseline-specific bounds from 400 component bootstrap draws, using quantiles 0.005/0.995 and RNG 424242 under the evaluator's Bonferroni construction. Describe this construction rather than calling it a conventional single-comparator or seed-independent 95% interval.\",\n    \"Boundary comparison: constant-history MAE is 0.06436711309859705 and independently shifted-history MAE is 0.06856952089296987. Compare these conditions to the matched model-holdout condition, never to a pooled A/B primary mean; show the same fixed baselines alongside them. The shifted condition does not retain an advantage over the column-mean reference in the available arrays. This limitation belongs in the main results and conclusion.\",\n    \"Use sealed, common targets for the primary claim, with uniform targets within row, uniform rows within episode, equal checkpoint weights, equal statistical-seed weights and equal primary-condition weights. All-unqueried losses are a supplementary descriptive view because their evaluated target set changes with the method's selected queries.\",\n    \"No matched final residual-off, unconditional residual, donor-score-only, support-gating, missingness-stratified or cross-leaderboard ablation is available. Training diagnostic code is not outer empirical evidence. Do not fill this gap with search comparisons or invented results.\"\n  ],\n  \"figure_plan\": [\n    \"Figure 1, early overview: distinguish historical fitting/calibration from a held-out model's permitted queried scores and sealed targets; show where low-rank prediction and local residual transfer combine.\",\n    \"Figure 2, main accuracy comparison: two aligned panels for model and heuristic-family holdouts, four budget categories and four fixed method series; retain all five comparators in the adjacent main table.\",\n    \"Figure 3, evidence boundary: one grouped comparison of intact, constant and column-shifted history on the matched model-holdout target protocol, with proposed, column-mean and variance-selected low-rank predictors.\"\n  ],\n  \"figure_specs\": [\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"method_overview\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"purpose\": \"Make the sparse score matrix, budgeted queries, historical donor calibration and sealed-target boundary immediately legible without suggesting target access or measured component attribution.\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"diagram\",\n      \"preferred_generator\": \"generate-concept-figure\",\n      \"group_by\": \"information_boundary\",\n      \"metric\": \"none; qualitative mechanism, not numerical evidence\",\n      \"split_by\": \"history-only fit and held-out-model inference\",\n      \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n      \"target_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"layout_rationale\": \"paper.sty sets a single 5.5-inch text column and 9-inch text height; full_width is therefore the same physical span as the column, not a two-column figure*. Use two vertically stacked panels, each with at most five labeled objects, so 9-point text remains readable. Place on page 1 or 2, targeting 2.0-2.3 inches of artwork plus a concise caption, about one third of a page. Separate history calibration from inference with a visible boundary; do not shrink to a half-column thumbnail.\",\n      \"allow_progression\": false,\n      \"max_panels\": 2,\n      \"max_panels_per_row\": 1,\n      \"max_categories_per_panel\": 5,\n      \"max_series_per_panel\": 2\n    },\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"budget_comparison\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"purpose\": \"Directly compare sealed-target error versus query budget for the studied method and fixed low-rank/linear references under both held-out conditions.\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"line_plot\",\n      \"preferred_generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"group_by\": \"method_family\",\n      \"metric\": \"fixed sealed-target mean absolute error in normalized score units; lower is better\",\n      \"split_by\": \"model/alias/duplicate holdout and heuristic-family/duplicate holdout\",\n      \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n      \"target_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"layout_rationale\": \"Use the full 5.5-inch single-column width for two side-by-side panels, at most two panels per row, one shared external legend, four budget ticks and four series: studied method, random-query low-rank, variance-query low-rank, linear ridge. Target 2.4-2.7 inches of artwork plus caption, roughly 0.4 page on pages 5-6. Use at least 8.5-point rendered labels and identical y limits. Seed standard deviations may be shown as descriptive error bars, not independent-sample confidence intervals; define any uncertainty drawn. If labels collide, stack the panels at the same width rather than shrinking text.\",\n      \"allow_progression\": false,\n      \"max_panels\": 2,\n      \"max_panels_per_row\": 2,\n      \"max_categories_per_panel\": 4,\n      \"max_series_per_panel\": 4\n    },\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"history_structure_controls\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"purpose\": \"Expose the intact-history advantage and its limitations under two matched data disruptions without presenting controls as mechanism-isolating ablations.\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"grouped_point\",\n      \"preferred_generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"group_by\": \"historical_data_condition\",\n      \"metric\": \"fixed sealed-target MAE averaged equally over budgets and statistical seeds\",\n      \"split_by\": \"none; all three conditions use the matched model-holdout protocol\",\n      \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n      \"target_width_fraction\": 0.9,\n      \"layout_rationale\": \"At 0.9 times the 5.5-inch column, a single panel can show three conditions and three series: studied method, column means, variance-query low-rank. Target 1.7-2.0 inches of artwork plus caption, roughly 0.3 page on pages 6-7. Use short labels Intact / Constant / Column-shifted and explain exact transforms in the caption; at least 9-point labels avoid a dense condition table disguised as a plot. A small horizontal offset resolves equal-valued points without suggesting different losses.\",\n      \"allow_progression\": false,\n      \"max_panels\": 1,\n      \"max_panels_per_row\": 1,\n      \"max_categories_per_panel\": 3,\n      \"max_series_per_panel\": 3\n    }\n  ],\n  \"supplementary_plan\": {\n    \"purpose\": \"After the references, provide reimplementation and audit depth from existing source and evaluator evidence, while leaving the principal comparisons, uncertainty construction, control failures and qualification limitations visible in the main paper.\",\n    \"planned_subsections\": [\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"Observed-score extraction and split provenance\",\n        \"grounded_content\": \"Document the pinned MTEB results and task-definition revisions, metric compatibility and bounded-score handling, modal revision/coverage rules, canonicalized duplicate components, heuristic-family construction, and disjoint search/final allocation. Use task/resources/final/manifest.json, task/resources/final/plan.json and task/evaluate_final.py as direct local provenance; do not assign them invented grounding IDs. Explain the current-license versus historical-license caveat and absence of generative-model evidence.\",\n        \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\", \"final.qualification.status\", \"final.qualification.outcome\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"Residual-transfer algorithm and historical calibration\",\n        \"grounded_content\": \"Expand the inspected selected src/program.py and RESIDUAL_METHOD.md into pseudocode and a parameter table: rank-four ridge ALS, auxiliary fit caps, two-level historical folds, observed donor targets, shared-anchor distances, kernel/overlap weights, weighted median, effective-count shrinkage, exact zero-blend fallback and tuning grid. Distinguish source-defined caps from measured runtime, and explain why approximate auxiliary fits and limited donor support do not ensure unbiased residuals or component-specific gains.\",\n        \"source_ids\": [\"result.best_correct_score\", \"final.evidence.episodes\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"Complete sealed-target comparisons and paired uncertainty\",\n        \"grounded_content\": \"Report all five baselines and the studied method by holdout, budget and statistical seed, deriving tables from the row/checkpoint arrays already in paper_data.json. Preserve method ordering from the evaluator. Give paired component-resampling pseudocode, equal aggregation weights, baseline-bound minima, finite 400-draw resolution, shared rows/components across seeds, and handling of failed episodes. Main-paper controls and uncertainty remain in the main paper, not only here.\",\n        \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\", \"final.metric.mae\", \"final.metric.baseline_mae\", \"final.metric.gain_ci_low\", \"final.metric.gain_ci_high\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"Changing-target diagnostic and disruption details\",\n        \"grounded_content\": \"Contrast all_unqueried_mae_by_row_checkpoint with sealed-target losses using only the retained arrays, explicitly labeling the former as a changing-target-set diagnostic. Document constant-history masks and independently shifted column value/mask pairs, matched original targets, and observed-count fields. Do not turn these into a new missingness generalization claim or a component ablation.\",\n        \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\", \"final.metric.constant_control_mae\", \"final.metric.permuted_control_mae\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"Reproducibility, configuration selection and incomplete evidence\",\n        \"grounded_content\": \"Map every proposed table/plot to a future reproducible extraction script over paper_data.json; record the existing evaluator entry point, requirements, final seeds, program/evaluator/data fingerprints and reset result. Briefly disclose adaptive search selection and a failed support-gating attempt from results/attempts_history.jsonl with no retained numeric performance, keeping it separate from final comparisons. Missing diagnostics leave its failure cause unknown; the two search scores are not paired final ablations. Commands, environment and actual measured durations may be reported only after inspection or verification, not estimated from task budgets.\",\n        \"source_ids\": [\"result.best_correct_score\", \"result.top_program_01\", \"result.top_program_02\", \"result.total_attempts\", \"result.total_evaluated_programs\", \"final.evidence.episodes\", \"final.metric.reset_max_abs_delta\"]\n      },\n      {\n        \"heading\": \"LLM Usage\",\n        \"grounded_content\": \"Provide the separate disclosure required by VENUE.md, limited to documented ideation, implementation assistance, reference-query planning, paper framing and subsequent writing assistance when actually performed. State that the numerical evidence comes from the frozen evaluator, not language-model-generated measurements. Disclose this strategy stage without claiming the later manuscript or figures were already generated.\",\n        \"source_ids\": [\"final.evidence.episodes\"]\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  \"forbidden_headings\": [\n    \"Search Progression\",\n    \"Campaign Phases\",\n    \"Research Timeline\",\n    \"Milestones\",\n    \"First Successful Result\",\n    \"Experiment A / Experiment B\",\n    \"Round-by-Round Improvements\",\n    \"Winner Selection as the Main Result\"\n  ],\n  \"forbidden_phrases\": [\n    \"first valid result\",\n    \"subsequent improvements\",\n    \"we then tried\",\n    \"later variants\",\n    \"next-best\",\n    \"correctness rate\",\n    \"correct and eligible\",\n    \"eligible\",\n    \"worker_01\",\n    \"worker_02\",\n    \"combined_score\",\n    \"rubric\",\n    \"state of the art\",\n    \"provably optimal benchmark selection\",\n    \"residual correction explains the gain\",\n    \"eight benchmarks are enough\",\n    \"generalizes to generative LLM evaluation\",\n    \"unscored attempts were worse\"\n  ],\n  \"notes_for_writer\": [\n    \"PRIVATE-ONLY EVIDENCE MAP: final.qualification.status/outcome/correct ground evaluation status; final.metric.* ground retained aggregate numbers; final.evidence.episodes grounds evaluator-computed losses and seeds, even though candidate_diagnostics_trust is unverified. Do not treat any candidate self-diagnostics as evidence. result.* sources describe search, not final performance. No study.* sources exist, so do not invent downstream selection statistics or study source IDs.\",\n    \"PRIVATE-ONLY SELECTION CONTEXT: the supplied result.best_correct_score points to the residual-transfer source. No original greedy-ALS predictor has a matched residual-off final result in the five-baseline suite. Preserve the original search comparator only as explicitly labeled search context where a direct record exists, never relabel final linear ridge or variance-query ALS as that incumbent. An unscored failed attempt is missing evidence, not a measured loss; do not infer a statistical, validity or adaptivity failure from missing gate diagnostics.\",\n    \"Reader-facing contribution list: a fully specified sparse-score estimator combining historical low-rank structure and local residual transfer; an observed-support study with common sealed targets under two holdout regimes and four budgets; and explicit empirical boundaries from historical-data disruptions. Do not claim firstness, a new theorem, or an isolated residual-component improvement.\",\n    \"Main organization: Introduction and problem scope; Related work; Budgeted score prediction and local residual transfer; Evaluation protocol; Accuracy across budgets and holdouts; Historical-structure controls; Discussion and limitations; Conclusion. Use descriptive condition names, not the evaluator's letter labels as visible experiment titles.\",\n    \"ICLR 2026 per VENUE.md: plan 8.5 substantive body pages, excluding references and appendix. Allocate 1.25 pages to title/abstract/introduction, 0.75 related work, 0.75 problem and information boundary, 1.75 method, 1.25 protocol, 1.75 results including controls, and 1.0 discussion/limitations/conclusion. Figure/table space is included in these allocations, not added on top. An eight-page body must end at least 75% full; never stretch spacing or invent experiments to fill it.\",\n    \"Spend available space on the ALS objective and inference equations, exact residual estimator, nested historical calibration, shared-target reasoning, component-resampling uncertainty, complete baseline table, and information-support limitations. Include an algorithm box and concise parameter table as explanatory content, not additional empirical results. If the grounding cannot sustain the minimum body length, report that rather than padding.\",\n    \"The inspected paper.sty specifies textwidth 5.5 inches (about 397.5 TeX points), textheight 9 inches and a single-column layout. full_width and column are the same maximum physical width; never assume a two-column template. Existing legacy comments do not verify official ICLR 2026 template provenance: the writer must verify format provenance before claiming submission compliance, without changing scientific scope.\",\n    \"Quantitative figures must be generated from retained arrays by a reproducible plotting script, exporting vector PDF and PNG at 300 DPI; LaTeX must include PDF whenever available. Use consistent colors, at least 8.5-9 point rendered labels and a shared legend. For raster-only figures require at least 2400 pixels across for full-width placement or 1200 for a true reduced-column placement; regenerate from source rather than upscale. Use a full-width overview at least 2400 pixels wide if no vector export exists.\",\n    \"Exactly one overview requests generate-concept-figure; orchestration, not this strategy agent, owns its later rendering. Preserve editable source and prompt provenance. Draw qualitative missing/observed/queried/target cell types without invented score values or illustrative numerical matrices masquerading as data. Historical calibration never sees held-out target answers, and querying does not use revealed target membership.\",\n    \"Method explanation must preserve what the source actually does: greedy selection sums posterior-variance gains over all columns and does not implement the discarded self-excluding transfer-only proposal. Score values affect new-row inference and donor weights; do not describe a score-adaptive selection policy when the retained source's selection depends on fitted factors and previously queried indices.\",\n    \"Main evidence limits: prediction is on previously observed leaderboard cells within a frozen queryable reservoir, not on genuinely unreported cells; benchmark availability can be informative; heuristic family labels do not certify hidden ancestry; same dataset/rows/components recur across seeds; target tasks/subsets need not be independent. No temporal, cross-leaderboard, generative-model, deployment-cost or minimum-sufficient-budget claim is supported.\",\n    \"The final evaluation is completed and qualified in this run. Explain the evaluated information-access and reset checks in reader-facing language without equating successful protocol completion with superiority or freedom from all leakage. If later audit/revision changes the final outcome to not_qualified, disclose it in the abstract, principal results, limitations, review and evidence audit; preserve retained metrics as descriptive only and leave unidentified failure causes unknown.\",\n    \"Use only existing references.bib keys with synchronized provenance and actually consulted content. Current metadata supports candidate positioning around sparse matrix factorization and neighbor methods, not uninspected claims about literature findings. Do not pad related work with irrelevant recommender applications, and do not search for or infer the prohibited paper or its citation network. Approved starter works can be used only after permissible consultation and grounded citation retrieval.\",\n    \"Plan the appendix after references with the substantive sections listed above plus the separate LLM Usage disclosure. Keep core control limitations, primary outcomes and uncertainty in the body. Do not invent future posthoc rankings; route later authoritative appendix-ranked material only when it actually arrives.\",\n    \"Maintain double-blind anonymity: no names, affiliations, acknowledgements, funding statements, personal links or internal filesystem paths in visible paper text. Private evidence mappings may retain local paths; public reproducibility instructions must be sanitized. Do not claim execution of new experiments, runtime validation, completed plots, final manuscript drafting or final-paper validation in this strategy stage.\"\n  ],\n  \"failure_reason\": \"\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
