{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_79","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"command -v pdflatex; command -v bibtex; command -v sudo; id; /tmp/paper-tools/bin/python -c 'import numpy,matplotlib,fitz,jsonschema;print(numpy.__version__,matplotlib.__version__,fitz.__version__)'; python - <<'PY'\nimport json\np=json.load(open('references_provenance.json'));print(p['count']);print([(r['citation_key'],r['consulted_content']) for r in p['references'] if r['consulted_content']\"'!='\"'abstract'])\nPY\nsed -n '21,28p' /tmp/reference_consultation.txt; cat .codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_rendered_si.py | head -150\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_79","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"command -v pdflatex; command -v bibtex; command -v sudo; id; /tmp/paper-tools/bin/python -c 'import numpy,matplotlib,fitz,jsonschema;print(numpy.__version__,matplotlib.__version__,fitz.__version__)'; python - <<'PY'\nimport json\np=json.load(open('references_provenance.json'));print(p['count']);print([(r['citation_key'],r['consulted_content']) for r in p['references'] if r['consulted_content']\"'!='\"'abstract'])\nPY\nsed -n '21,28p' /tmp/reference_consultation.txt; cat .codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_rendered_si.py | head -150\"","aggregated_output":"/tmp/paper-tools/lib/python3.12/site-packages/fitz/__init__.py:17: FutureWarning: The `fitz` API is deprecated and will be removed in future. Use `import pymupdf` instead.\n  warnings.warn(\n2.5.5 3.11.2 2.1\n25\n[]\nKoenker2001 Quantile Regression \n Quantile regression, as introduced by Koenker and Bassett (1978), may be viewed as an extension of classical least squares estimation of conditional mean models to the estimation of an ensemble of models for several conditional quantile functions. The central special case is the median regression estimator which minimizes a sum of absolute errors. Other conditional quantile functions are estimated by minimizing an asymmetrically weighted sum of absolute errors. Quantile regression methods are illustrated with applications to models for CEO pay, food expenditure, and infant birthweight.\nStone1977 Consistent Nonparametric Regression \n Let $(X, Y)$ be a pair of random variables such that $X$ is $\\mathbb{R}^d$-valued and $Y$ is $\\mathbb{R}^{d'}$-valued. Given a random sample $(X_1, Y_1), \\cdots, (X_n, Y_n)$ from the distribution of $(X, Y)$, the conditional distribution $P^Y(\\bullet \\mid X)$ of $Y$ given $X$ can be estimated nonparametrically by $\\hat{P}_n^Y(A \\mid X) = \\sum^n_1 W_{ni}(X)I_A(Y_i)$, where the weight function $W_n$ is of the form $W_{ni}(X) = W_{ni}(X, X_1, \\cdots, X_n), 1 \\leqq i \\leqq n$. The weight function $W_n$ is called a probability weight function if it is nonnegative and $\\sum^n_1 W_{ni}(X) = 1$. Associated with $\\hat{P}_n^Y(\\bullet \\mid X)$ in a natural way are nonparametric estimators of conditional expectations, variances, covariances, standard deviations, correlations and quantiles and nonparametric approximate Bayes rules in prediction and multiple classification problems. Consistency of a sequence $\\{W_n\\}$ of weight functions is defined and sufficient conditions for consistency are obtained. When applied to sequences of probability weight functions, these conditions are both necessary and sufficient. Consistent sequences of probability weight functions defined in terms of nearest neighbors are constructed. The results are applied to verify the consistency of the estimators of the various quantities discussed above and the consistency in Bayes risk of the approximate Bayes rules.\nBengio2004 No Unbiased Estimator of the Variance of K-Fold Cross-Validation \n Most machine learning researchers perform quantitative experiments to estimate generalization error and compare the performance of different algorithms (in particular, their proposed algorithm). In order to be able to draw statistically convincing conclusions, it is important to estimate the uncertainty of such estimates. This paper studies the very commonly used K-fold cross-validation estimator of generalization performance. The main theorem shows that there exists no universal (valid under all distributions) unbiased estimator of the variance of K-fold cross-validation. The analysis that accompanies this result is based on the eigen-decomposition of the covariance matrix of errors, which has only three different eigenvalues corresponding to three degrees of freedom of the matrix and three components of the total variance. This analysis helps to better understand the nature of the problem and how it can make naive estimators (that don’t take into account the error correlations due to the overlap between training and test sets) grossly underestimate variance. This is confirmed by numerical experiments in which the three components of the variance are compared when the difficulty of the learning problem and the number of folds are varied.\nHoerl1970 Ridge Regression: Biased Estimation for Nonorthogonal Problems \n In multiple regression it is shown that parameter estimates based on minimum residual sum of squares have a high probability of being unsatisfactory, if not incorrect, if the prediction vectors are not orthogonal. Proposed is an estimation procedure based on adding small positive quantities to the diagonal of X′X. Introduced is the ridge trace, a method for showing in two dimensions the effects of nonorthogonality. It is then shown how to augment X′X to obtain biased estimates with smaller mean square error.\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Validate rendered Supplementary Information in a compiled paper PDF.\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport argparse\nimport re\nimport shutil\nimport subprocess\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\n\ndef _letter_spaced_word_pattern(word: str) -> str:\n    return r\"[ \\t]*\".join(re.escape(character) for character in word)\n\n\nSUPPLEMENT_HEADING_RE = re.compile(\n    rf\"(?mi)^[ \\t]*(?:(?:appendix[ \\t]+)?[A-Z0-9]+(?:\\.\\d+)*[.:]?[ \\t]+)?\"\n    rf\"{_letter_spaced_word_pattern('supplementary')}[ \\t]+\"\n    rf\"{_letter_spaced_word_pattern('information')}[ \\t]*$\",\n)\nREFERENCE_HEADING_RE = re.compile(\n    rf\"(?mi)^[ \\t]*(?:{_letter_spaced_word_pattern('references')}|\"\n    rf\"{_letter_spaced_word_pattern('bibliography')})[ \\t]*$\"\n)\n\n\ndef parse_args() -> argparse.Namespace:\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(\n        description=\"Validate rendered Supplementary Information in a PDF.\"\n    )\n    parser.add_argument(\"--pdf\", required=True)\n    return parser.parse_args()\n\n\ndef validate_rendered_supplementary_information(page_texts: list[str]) -> list[str]:\n    supplement_page_index = next(\n        (\n            index\n            for index, page_text in enumerate(page_texts)\n            if SUPPLEMENT_HEADING_RE.search(page_text)\n        ),\n        None,\n    )\n    if supplement_page_index is None:\n        return [\"compiled paper is missing the Supplementary Information heading\"]\n\n    heading_page_text = page_texts[supplement_page_index]\n    heading_match = SUPPLEMENT_HEADING_RE.search(heading_page_text)\n    assert heading_match is not None\n    reference_page_indices = [\n        index\n        for index, page_text in enumerate(page_texts[:supplement_page_index])\n        if REFERENCE_HEADING_RE.search(page_text)\n    ]\n    errors: list[str] = []\n    if not reference_page_indices:\n        errors.append(\"compiled paper is missing the References heading before SI\")\n    heading_page_prefix = heading_page_text[: heading_match.start()]\n    prefix_lines = [line.strip() for line in heading_page_prefix.splitlines() if line.strip()]\n    earlier_header_lines = {\n        line.strip()\n        for page_text in page_texts[:supplement_page_index]\n        for line in page_text.splitlines()[:2]\n        if line.strip()\n    }\n    substantive_prefix_lines = [\n        line\n        for line in prefix_lines\n        if re.fullmatch(r\"(?:page\\s+)?\\d+\", line, re.IGNORECASE) is None\n        and line not in earlier_header_lines\n    ]\n    if substantive_prefix_lines:\n        errors.append(\n            \"compiled Supplementary Information must start on a separate page after References\"\n        )\n\n    supplement_chunks = [\n        heading_page_text[heading_match.end() :],\n        *page_texts[supplement_page_index + 1 :],\n    ]\n    substantive_chunks: list[str] = []\n    for chunk in supplement_chunks:\n        later_references = REFERENCE_HEADING_RE.search(chunk)\n        if later_references is not None:\n            substantive_chunks.append(chunk[: later_references.start()])\n            break\n        substantive_chunks.append(chunk)\n    supplement_text = \"\\n\".join(substantive_chunks)\n    visible_character_count = len(re.sub(r\"\\s+\", \" \", supplement_text).strip())\n    if visible_character_count < 100:\n        errors.append(\n            \"compiled Supplementary Information must contain at least 100 visible characters\"\n        )\n    return errors\n\n\ndef main() -> int:\n    args = parse_args()\n    pdf_path = Path(args.pdf)\n    if not pdf_path.is_file():\n        raise SystemExit(f\"Missing compiled PDF: {pdf_path}\")\n    pdftotext = shutil.which(\"pdftotext\")\n    if pdftotext is None:\n        raise SystemExit(\"Missing required command: pdftotext\")\n    extracted = subprocess.run(\n        [pdftotext, \"-layout\", str(pdf_path), \"-\"],\n        check=False,\n        capture_output=True,\n        text=True,\n    )\n    if extracted.returncode != 0:\n        raise SystemExit(f\"pdftotext failed: {extracted.stderr.strip()}\")\n    page_texts = extracted.stdout.split(\"\\f\")\n    errors = validate_rendered_supplementary_information(page_texts)\n    if errors:\n        raise SystemExit(\"Rendered Supplementary Information validation failed: \" + \"; \".join(errors))\n    print(\"Validated rendered Supplementary Information.\")\n    return 0\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    raise SystemExit(main())\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
