{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_106","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"/tmp/apply_patch <<'PATCH'\n*** Begin Patch\n*** Update File: paper.tex\n@@\n-Our contributions are a fully specified budgeted predictor, an evaluation with fixed targets and dependence-aware paired uncertainty, and an empirical account of where the observed advantage disappears. The underlying factorization, local weighting, and design principles have established antecedents; novelty is not claimed for those primitives. The available comparisons identify the performance of the complete configuration, not an optimal selector or a uniquely effective correction mechanism.\n+Our contributions are a fully specified budgeted predictor, a fixed-target evaluation with dependence-aware paired uncertainty, and a measured failure boundary. Factorization, local weighting, and design have established antecedents; we do not claim novelty for these primitives or optimality of the complete configuration.\n@@\n-These observations answer a restricted budget question: more queried columns improve the reported aggregate over the tested range. They do not identify a universally adequate \"'$k$. No application-specific error tolerance was specified, budgets beyond the observed range were not measured, and a task/subset column is not a uniform unit of runtime. A cost-sensitive evaluation policy would require per-column evaluation costs not present here. Even the acquisition surrogate optimizes total linear predictive variance rather than the sealed-target MAE used for reporting.\n+More queried columns improve the reported aggregate over the tested range, but do not identify a universally adequate $k$. No application-specific tolerance or per-column execution costs were specified, and larger budgets were not measured. Moreover, the acquisition surrogate optimizes linear predictive variance, not sealed-target MAE.\n@@\n-The strongest conclusion is about retrospective prediction of '\"\\\\emph{previously observed} MTEB task/subset scores in a frozen matrix. An observed-cell target set can favor models and tasks whose reporting patterns already provide useful overlap. Availability itself may depend on performance, resources, or evaluation priorities. The experiment does not identify a missingness mechanism or validate prediction of genuinely unreported cells. Public metric-range normalization also does not make equal errors equally important across applications. Dependence among task subsets means the column count should not be read as the number of independent scientific tests.\n+The conclusion concerns \\\\emph{previously observed} scores in a frozen matrix. Reporting patterns can favor models and tasks with useful overlap, and availability may depend on performance or resources. The experiment neither identifies the missingness mechanism nor validates genuinely unreported-cell prediction. Normalization does not equalize application importance, and dependent task subsets are not independent scientific tests.\n@@\n-The conclusion is deliberately bounded. Disrupting historical column alignment removes the advantage over column means, and the comparisons do not isolate a residual-specific causal effect. Observed-cell prediction, heuristic family separation, and one frozen embedding leaderboard do not establish recovery of genuinely missing scores or transfer to generative models. Nor do they determine a generally sufficient evaluation budget. The useful finding is a measured configuration-level advantage together with an explicit failure boundary: historical score structure can support compact evaluation, but its suitability must itself be examined rather than assumed.\n+Disrupting historical column alignment removes the advantage over column means, and the comparisons do not isolate a residual-specific causal effect. Observed-cell prediction with heuristic family separation does not establish recovery of genuinely missing scores, transfer to generative models, or a generally sufficient budget. The finding is a configuration-level advantage with an explicit failure boundary: historical score structure can support compact evaluation, but its suitability must be examined rather than assumed.\n*** End Patch\nPATCH\npdflatex -recorder -no-shell-escape -interaction=nonstopmode -halt-on-error paper.tex > /tmp/paper_manual_compile.log 2>&1; tail -3 /tmp/paper_manual_compile.log\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
